      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Myocardial infarction induces left ventricular (LV) wall motion abnormalities during isovolumic relaxation (IVR) and may potentially alter intraventricular flow during this period. This study evaluated whether 2-dimensional color Doppler measurements of intraventricular flow during IVR were able to identify LV dysfunction caused by coronary artery disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Patients with single-vessel coronary artery disease and posterior wall infarction (21 patients) or anterior wall infarction (27 patients) were included. Eighteen healthy persons served as a control group. LV function was examined by 2-dimensional echocardiography, 2-dimensional color Doppler, and pulsed Doppler techniques."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "All normal persons (23.6 +/- 10.9 cm/s) and patients with posterior infarction (19.6 +/- 9.3 cm/s) had flow propagation towards LV apex during IVR. Patients with anterior wall infarction had reversed flow direction (-12.2 +/- 8.7 cm/s, P <.001). The echocardiographic wall motion score index of the 4 apical segments correlated well with flow velocities (r = -0.78, P <.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Reversed flow propagation during IVR may become a sensitive clinical marker of regional ischemia."
        }
      ]},
